analysis:
All four changes are test-only code under provider-swift/Tests/ClusterInstalledSessionChecks. I found no production code, key handling, attestation, WebSocket, cache or authorization path modified.

InstalledBatchLanesCheck.swift adds a Gemma 4 tensor-parallel block (head lines 218-241 of the change). It checks:
- the default lane count is 1;
- widths 2/4/8 propagate through the sanitized owner child;
- malformed overrides stay at one lane;
- arithmetic env values reach the native worker;
- committed-token logprobs are captured.
The existing tests that the sanitized owner environment forwards no DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES or unrelated variables are kept. These are control-strengthening tests relevant to TB-003 and TB-007. The checks do not weaken any assertion that guards the environment boundary.

InstalledSessionCheck.swift has two changes:
- The relay test now uses an OS-assigned ephemeral loopback port, with up to 8 retries, and excludes the cluster ports 47421-47423. This avoids colliding with a live pair on the same Mac. The helper binds 127.0.0.1 only (INADDR_LOOPBACK), and `relay.port` is used afterward. This is a test-hygiene improvement with no security impact. Whether ClusterLocalRelay exposes a `port` property is outside the supplied source (it is not visible in the diff); that is a build concern, not a security one.
- logprobModeAdmission now includes the Gemma 4 adapter as supporting logprobs only in tensor-parallel mode.

The logprob assertions are test expectations only. The production admission logic that decides logprob support (DistributedResidentExecutionProfile and the adapter's lane/logprob capability) is not in the supplied evidence. If the production change that enables Gemma TP logprobs and lanes is in another PR unit, it deserves review for T-041 and A-001 exposure. Logprob capture can leak additional information to consumers, but consumers legitimately request it.

No findings. No deeper review is needed for these test files.
